1	# E2E Test Environment and Fixtures
2	
3	Apply this reference to the runtime the tests execute against: how the suite obtains a running system, drives it, keeps it deterministic, and shares expensive setup. These concerns are separate from how an individual test case is written (see [conventions.md](./conventions.md)).
4	
5	## Drive the Real System Under Test
6	
7	An e2e suite's value is fidelity: it must fail when a real user or client would fail. Drive the system through the same client it exposes to the outside world — a browser for a web UI, a device driver for a mobile app, the real protocol client for a service — never a hand-rolled substitute that can silently drift from real behavior.
8	
9	**Guidelines:**
10	
11	- MUST exercise the system through the client it actually exposes (browser driver, device driver, or the real SDK/protocol client), not a hand-rolled request that bypasses the real session, negotiation, or transport.
12	- MUST assert against a real running build, not mocked-out internals; mocking the thing under test turns an e2e test back into a unit test.
13	- SHOULD connect one shared client/context per test file (in a before-all hook) and close it in an after-all hook; per-test clients re-negotiate for no benefit.
14	
15	**Example — one shared protocol client per file:**
16	
17	```ts
18	import { afterAll, beforeAll } from "vitest";
19	
20	let client: Client;
21	
22	beforeAll(async () => {
23	  client = new Client({ name: "e2e", version: "0.0.0" });
24	  await client.connect(new StreamableHTTPClientTransport(new URL(endpoint)));
25	});
26	
27	afterAll(async () => {
28	  await client.close();
29	});
30	```
31	
32	## Own the Server Lifecycle in One Place
33	
34	The suite needs a running system before the first test. Own that lifecycle in one place — a global setup, or the runner's built-in server manager — and wait for readiness by polling an observable signal, never a fixed sleep. Individual test files import the base URL and never boot servers themselves.
35	
36	**Guidelines:**
37	
38	- MUST start the system under test from one place (a global-setup module, or the runner's built-in server block) and expose its base URL to the test files.
39	- MUST poll a readiness signal (a health request succeeding, the port accepting connections) with a bounded deadline instead of sleeping a guessed duration.
40	- MUST tear the server down after the run when the suite started it.
41	- SHOULD prefer the runner's built-in server manager when it has one (for example Playwright's `webServer`), and hand-roll a spawn-and-poll global setup only when the runner has no equivalent.
42	
43	**Example — spawn, poll until ready, tear down:**
44	
45	```ts
46	import { spawn } from "node:child_process";
47	
48	const PORT = 3100;
49	// Exported so test files can import the base URL instead of booting a server.
50	export const BASE_URL = `http://localhost:${PORT}`;
51	let server: ReturnType<typeof spawn>;
52	
53	export async function setup() {
54	  server = spawn("node", ["./server", "--port", String(PORT)], {
55	    stdio: "ignore",
56	  });
57	
58	  const deadline = Date.now() + 60_000;
59	  while (Date.now() < deadline) {
60	    try {
61	      if ((await fetch(BASE_URL, { signal: AbortSignal.timeout(1_000) })).ok) {
62	        return;
63	      }
64	    } catch {
65	      // not accepting connections yet
66	    }
67	    await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 500));
68	  }
69	  throw new Error("Server not ready in time.");
70	}
71	
72	// Tear the server down after the run (see the guideline above).
73	export async function teardown() {
74	  server?.kill();
75	}
76	```
77	
78	## Deterministic by Default
79	
80	The suite runs on every merge, so a dependency on a live third-party site makes CI fail on someone else's outage. Exercise failure paths with deterministic, offline-safe inputs, and push journeys that genuinely need the external network into a manual bucket instead of writing flaky automated tests.
81	
82	**Guidelines:**
83	
